  "result": "No. A language model does not remember earlier conversations on its own.\n\n**Why it seems true:** A chatbot appears to remember. It does not. The app sends the whole conversation again with each new message. The model reads that text and writes the next reply. When the call ends, the model keeps nothing.\n\n**Everyday example:** Think of a helper who loses all memory every time you leave the room. Each time you come back, you hand them a notebook. They read it, then answer. They know only what is in the notebook.\n\n**For your assistant:** A student talks to it on Monday. On the next Monday, the model knows nothing about that talk. Your code must do three things:\n\n1. Save the important facts, for example \"Sam finished laser cutter training\".\n2. Load those facts at the start of the next session.\n3. Put them in the text sent to the model.\n\nThis is the \"what to save between sessions\" decision in your mission.\n\n**Question:** Last week's chat with a student has 5,000 words. You have a small budget. Would you reload all of it next week, or save something shorter? Why?",
